--TEST--
`Core\Bytes::pack` and `Core\Bytes::unpack` share one format grammar and one sink, so a tainted format is refused where the call is written by every route it can arrive by, while the same grammar written plain packs and unpacks — `rule:security/sink-predicate` and `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R11
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R11 names a `Core\Bytes::pack` format one of four grammars, and
// `rule:security/sink-predicate`'s corollary makes each of them a `Qual::Sink` wherever it is
// declared: these bytes become the instruction deciding how many octets are
// read and how they are widened, so a caller who chooses them chooses the
// read. Both rows spell the parameter `CoreTy::Text(Qual::Sink)`
// (`crates/nvs-stdlib/src/bytes.rs:289` and `:298`), which makes every refusal
// below an ordinary assignability failure at the call site rather than a rule
// either member checks for itself — and that is the point being pinned: there
// is no launderer in front of this sink, so nothing a caller can write in the
// format position gets past it.
//
// The agreement shape (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): one question asked of the
// writer and the reader together, over the routes a taint arrives by, so a
// member that grew its own opinion of the format fails here while still
// looking right on its own line.
class Wire {
    public static function format(): tainted string {
        return "N";
    }
}

tainted string $t = "N";
secret tainted string $st = "N";
array<tainted string> $bag = [$t];

// The accepted side first, so this is a bound with both sides named rather
// than a pair of members that refuse everything: the same grammar, written
// plain, both writes and reads.
string $plain = "N";
bytes $written = Core\Bytes::pack($plain, 1);
array<mixed> $read = Core\Bytes::unpack($written, $plain);
echo Core\Arr::count($read);

// Directly, on each member, because the sink is declared twice and a hole in
// one row says nothing about the other.
bytes $a = Core\Bytes::pack($t, 1);
echo Core\Bytes::length($a);
array<mixed> $b = Core\Bytes::unpack($written, $t);
echo Core\Arr::count($b);

// Composed: taint travels through concatenation on its own axis, so a format
// assembled around a tainted fragment is tainted whatever the fragments were.
bytes $c = Core\Bytes::pack("N" . $t, 1, 2);
echo Core\Bytes::length($c);

// Declared one frame away. The call site reads the member's return type, which
// is where a sink that only looked at variables would miss it.
array<mixed> $d = Core\Bytes::unpack($written, Wire::format());
echo Core\Arr::count($d);

// Out of a container, where the qualifier is on the element type.
bytes $e = Core\Bytes::pack($bag[0], 1);
echo Core\Bytes::length($e);

// Composed with `secret`. Two axes, and neither launders the other: this one
// is refused for its taint, and the message says so by naming both.
array<mixed> $f = Core\Bytes::unpack($written, $st);
echo Core\Arr::count($f);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
